# Scratch is opt-IN, not tracking.
#
# Everything in scripts/ is tracked by default; only scripts/local/ is ignored.
# Put throwaway spikes, one-shot backfills and experiments there. Anything that
# a test imports, that ./test.sh or CI invokes, or that anyone is expected to
# run twice is load-bearing and belongs at the top level, where git and ruff
# both see it.
#
# ── why this file exists at all ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# It is NOT here to hide scripts. It replaced the root-level glob
# "backend/scripts/*", which ruff's gitignore-respecting walker (the `ignore`
# crate) misapplied to the app/ and tests/ walks when run from backend/,
# silently shrinking lint+format discovery to 4 files. ("N files already
# formatted" with a tiny N is the tell — the count should be in the low
# hundreds and growing.) A per-directory .gitignore only ever applies to walks
# inside scripts/, so ruff discovery stays correct everywhere. Keep the rule
# here; do not move it back to the root.
#
# ── why it inverted (2026-08-12) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# This file used to be `*` plus a negation per tracked script. That default was
# backwards: forgetting a negation made a script untracked AND silently skipped
# by ruff, and neither absence is visible in `git status` — an ignored file
# looks exactly like a file that isn't there.
#
# It shipped the same CI failure twice (1029ab1, 6b8f225): a commit added a
# tracked test importing an untracked script, which passes ./test.sh locally
# (the file is on disk) and fails CI at import on a fresh checkout with
# ModuleNotFoundError. The allowlist also could not protect anki_archive/ —
# "tracked files override gitignore" only covers files already tracked when `*`
# landed, so a NEW archive script was swallowed silently.
#
# Under the inverted rule both failures are structurally impossible: a new
# script shows up as untracked in `git status`, and forgetting to move a
# throwaway into local/ costs a lint pass, not a green-locally/red-in-CI commit.
local/
